\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{Model collapse and canary metrics.}
Iterative training collapse was formalized by \citet{shumailov2024curse} and \citet{alemohammad2024mad}, who showed that recursively replacing real data with model-generated text causes progressive tail erosion.
Theoretical analyses have sharpened these results: \citet{dohmatob2024tale} recast collapse as a change in scaling-law exponents, and \citet{dohmatob2025strong} proved that collapse can occur even when synthetic data constitutes a vanishingly small fraction of the training set.
Several works have since proposed monitoring metrics that precede or accompany degradation: \citet{du2026geometric} observe that calibration error rises before accuracy degrades; \citet{du2025scales} report ECE increases of 75\% at 30\% synthetic contamination before accuracy drops; and \citet{gambetta2024surplexity} introduces surplexity as a simultaneous collapse detector and data filter.
Other approaches track spectral divergence~\cite{sigma2026spectral}, topological features~\cite{kalinowski2026topology}, or center-edge sampling dynamics~\cite{dces2026counter}.
Yet none of these canary studies apply differencing tests to distinguish genuine temporal precedence from spurious co-trending, cross-validate across multiple statistical methods, or correct for false discovery rate when testing multiple metric pairs.

\paragraph{Early-warning signals and lead-lag methodology.}
The theoretical foundation for canary-based prediction originates in ecology: \citet{scheffer2009early} established that systems approaching tipping points exhibit generic early-warning signals, while \citet{boettiger2012limits} quantified detection limits in short time series---a constraint directly relevant to iterative training, where generation counts ($T{=}5$--$20$) are far smaller than typical ecological datasets.
The standard econometric framework for testing temporal precedence is Granger causality~\cite{granger1969investigating}, with extensions reviewed by \citet{shojaie2022granger}; however, standard Granger tests applied to non-stationary series produce inflated rejection rates~\cite{granger1974spurious}, and the Toda--Yamamoto procedure~\cite{toda1995statistical} addresses this at the cost of reduced degrees of freedom that become severe in short series.
Despite wide use in economics, these methods are largely absent from the model collapse literature, which relies on visual inspection or naive cross-correlation.

\paragraph{Analytical robustness.}
The broader issue of researcher degrees of freedom in ML has received increasing attention: \citet{bell2022modeling} introduced multiverse analysis, \citet{coretta2023multidimensional} demonstrated through a many-analysts design that 46 independent teams reached substantially different conclusions on identical data, and \citet{kapoor2024reforms} synthesized these concerns into a consensus checklist for ML-based science.
Our work applies this multiverse perspective to model collapse: five statistical methods across multiple contamination regimes produce conclusions ranging from universal significance to complete null on the same dataset.
No prior work bridges the collapse monitoring and econometric lead-lag literatures with systematic multi-method cross-validation, differencing diagnostics, false discovery correction, and regime-dependent calibration.

\section{Discussion and Conclusion}\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Granger precedence is not causation.}
Granger causality tests predictive improvement, not true causation~\cite{granger1969investigating,shojaie2022granger}: an unobserved process could independently drive both token entropy and diversity metrics.
Our central claim is scoped accordingly---``token entropy Granger-precedes diversity collapse''---and bivariate tests cannot exclude confounders; multivariate VAR models would be required to test for confounders directly.

\paragraph{Practical utility paradox.}
The framework works best where it is least needed: at $\alpha{\geq}0.75$, PermG reliably detects canary precedence, but degradation is already severe; at $\alpha{\leq}0.45$, where early warning would be most valuable, detection remains null.
However, in realistic pipelines, contamination $\alpha$ is unknown and typically increases over time as web-scraped corpora accumulate machine-generated text.
A continuous monitoring dashboard would trigger when $\alpha$ crosses the detectability threshold, converting the static limitation into a temporal advantage.
The regime map itself is actionable: practitioners who know $\alpha{<}0.25$ should invest in data provenance rather than metric monitoring.

\paragraph{Four-layer detection taxonomy.}
Our results suggest a layered taxonomy ordered by onset threshold:
(1)~distributional dimensionality (effective rank, onset $\alpha{\approx}0.10$);
(2)~distributional instability (entropy variance, suggestive at $\alpha{\approx}0.25$);
(3)~causal coupling (Granger-type, onset $\alpha_c{\approx}0.50$); and
(4)~dynamical stability (AC1, suggestive but not conclusive at $T{=}11$).
The 40-percentage-point gap between structural distortion (Layer~1) and causal evidence (Layer~3) constitutes the detection phase transition.
Layers~1 and~3 are established; Layers~2 and~4 require longer generation protocols ($T{\geq}50$; \citealt{dakos2012methods}).

\paragraph{Synchronous collapse at $\alpha{=}0.60$.}
The non-monotonic dip at $\alpha{=}0.60$ (PermG fwd below both $\alpha{=}0.50$ and $\alpha{=}0.75$) is consistent with the $S \times R$ prediction (\S\ref{sec:theory}): relaxation time matching drives $R \to 0$, so entropy and diversity respond within the same generation despite rising signal strength.
This creates a four-regime structure---blind, onset, synchronous dip, detection---rather than the binary boundary assumed by simpler models.

\paragraph{Method recommendation.}
The primary value of the multi-method framework is the systematic comparison it enables.
By applying five methods with different inductive biases, the framework reveals why each succeeds or fails: naive cross-correlation inflates FPR via reverse-directional associations; differenced cross-correlation cannot separate forward from reverse; threshold onset lacks power at $T{=}11$; Toda--Yamamoto achieves moderate detection (3--4/8) but is sensitive to augmentation order.
This process of elimination converges on PermG as the recommended method: zero null-control FPR, consistent detection at $\alpha{\geq}0.50$, and clean directional separation (24/24 forward, 0/24 reverse at $\alpha{=}1.0$ on WikiText).

\paragraph{Conclusion.}
Lead-lag analysis in iterative training collapse is far more fragile than assumed: method choice alone inflates spurious-signal rates to 75\% null-control FPR, first-differencing exposes canary$\to$perplexity associations as entirely spurious, and regime dependence causes the same test to shift from significant to null.
PermG identifies token entropy as a one-generation-ahead canary for diversity collapse, validated across 15 model-seed combinations spanning six families (345M--6.9B) with detection in 13 cases (87\%).
Directional asymmetry is domain-dependent (unidirectional on WikiText, bidirectional on C4), and canary-based predictive cessation reduces peak perplexity by 58\%.
Extended discussion of cross-architecture generalization, domain-dependent directionality, token entropy versus perplexity, NLP-specific constraints, and future directions appears in Appendix~\ref{app:extended_discussion}.

